  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CAFOD Humanitarian Leadership Trainee Project</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CAFOD is a member of the Consortium of British Humanitarian Agencies (CBHA). The overall objective of CBHA is &#x201c;To reduce suffering, mortality and morbidity in conflicts and natural disasters by strengthening the capacity and ability of the &#x201c;third pillar&#x201d; &#x2013; the NGO sector - to deliver appropriate high quality, effective and timely humanitarian responses.&#x201d;  

Amongst other things this will be achieved through capacity building which seeks to develop capacity within the CBH Agencies and the wider sector to increase the numbers and competencies of potential leaders, and increase the overall humanitarian skills and knowledge base of existing staff working in emergencies. One of the vehicles to achieve this is through the Humanitarian Leadership Trainee programme. Save the Children is the lead agency for the consortium on humanitarian leadership development.

The Humanitarian Leadership Trainee Programme is focused on bringing new leadership talent into the humanitarian sector. It is aimed at bringing new people into the humanitarian sector as well as enabling CBH Agencies to provide an exciting development opportunity for their most talented staff. 

CAFOD will participate in the Humanitarian Leadership Training Programme by hosting two trainees in its Kenya Nairobi Office. One trainee will be recruited in September 2010 and another one in the February 2011 intake. Each trainee will spend six months in Nairobi office and another six months on field placement with one of CAFOD&apos;s partners in the Horn and East Africa region. Save the Children will lead, develop the curricula and training programme for the trainees in consultation with member agencies.
The expected results of this programme will be to drive up minimum standards of staff leadership development across the sector and allow the CBHA to tailor courses to its member agencies&#x2019; specific needs. Furthermore, it is expected to address the lack of leadership in the sector and thereby improve the response to emergencies. CAFOD is not obliged to employ the trainees at the end of their training.</narrative>
  </description>
